part of the way in which African  Americans were kept as second-class  citizens in America was by denying them  access to guns I'm Tracy Oppenheimer  fries and TV and I'm here with Adam  Winkler author of gun fight the battle  over the right to bear arms in America  was a launching point what prompted you  to write gun fight well in 2008 when the  supreme court decided the Heller case I  got calls from so many reporters and  what does this mean for the future of  gun control this mean the end of gun  control laws as we know it and so I  wanted to tell the story the amazing  story of America's complicated history  with guns about the founding fathers  having gun laws that were more  restrictive than the NRA would like  today and sort of cast in a new light  the famed American gun culture and the  DC vs Heller case after it was resolved  what did that mean for gun owners well  it means that every gun control law now  has to pass constitutional muster has to  go through the courts and has to be  ruled to be constitutional by  solidifying the right to bear arms in  the American Constitution it provides a  way that we can sort of break the  political stalemate over guns and you  mentioned the NRA I know you said that  the NRA didn't want the Supreme Court to  make a decision even if it would be anti  gun control why would that be one of the  most interesting things about the Heller  case was the vehement opposition of the  NRA to the case ever being brought now  their stated reason was that they were  afraid of losing they didn't want the  supreme court to reject their view of  the Second Amendment especially this  supreme court with a majority of  conservative justices some of the  libertarian lawyers involved in the case  confided in me that they thought maybe  the NRA was also afraid of winning that  they thrive on crisis-driven fundraising  appeals that Warren gun owners the  government's coming to take your guns  you said in your book that gun controls  as much a part of American history  regarding guns as a Second Amendment is  what did you mean by that well we think  of gun control as a modern 20th century  invention but we've had gun control  since the founding fathers the founding  fathers had restrictive gun laws that in  part were race-based racially  discriminatory banning slaves were free  blacks from possessing guns this is one  of the most interesting things I found  in researching gunfight which was the  close connections  between gun-control historically and  racism part of the way in which  african-americans were kept as  second-class citizens in America was by  denying them access to guns if you could  talk about gun grabbers a little bit  what are they well the gun grabbers are  those on the Left who are seeking to get  rid of all the guns in America who deny  the existence of one of our oldest most  established constitutional rights  because they want to get rid of all the  guns and often they'll support silly and  ineffective laws that really have no  chance of reducing gun violence I liked  one of your quotes that you said  disarmament is no more likely to succeed  than prohibition or the war on drugs  could you talk a little bit about the  comparison between these reforms we  should learn from our past we've tried  through prohibition and the war on drugs  to get rid of things that are small easy  to conceal and that people love very  dearly and they've been terrible  failures I think if we tried to get rid  of all the guns in America it would  similarly be a failure and probably have  a lot of bad consequences as well  prohibition led to huge increases in  organized crime and criminal violence  the war on drugs has done pretty much  the same there's 280 million guns in  America they're not going anywhere one  thing I do like to point out when we  look internationally is that in America  we have high rates of gun ownership and  high rates of criminal violence there's  a lot of countries that are high rates  of gun ownership and low rates of  criminal violence Switzerland Israel  countries that have very low rates of  criminal violence I think America is a  violent Society not because of our guns  but because of our culture and yeah I  mean we all know that complete  disarmament is unrealistic so why do the  advocates keep pushing for it well and  then on the same time on the other side  we know that getting rid of gun control  entirely is unrealistic we want to have  laws that restrict the access of guns by  felons by people who don't have or  mentally ill by people who are drug  addicts I want to move the debate beyond  the extremes on both sides and recognize  that we have an individual right to bear  arms that many forms of gun control can  still be constitutional but we should  look carefully and closely at those laws  to make sure that they're effective  before we pass laws that in the end just  end up harassing lawful gun owners and  don't do anything to really reduce  criminal violence the more guns less  crime argument now do you think that  this is important to consider with going  control eyes one thing is crystal clear  we've had 30 years of liberalisation of  concealed carry laws and we've had 30  years of drastic reductions in criminal  violence I don't make the argument that  those things are necessarily connected  but one thing we certainly know is that  liberalizing access to guns has not led  to huge spikes and violence that many in  the gun control community suggested  would happen you talked about Ron Paul a  little bit and do you think he's had a  big impact well I don't think he's had a  huge impact but one of the great stories  about Ron Paul's involvement in this is  that the Heller case challenged a law  passed in nineteen seventy-six and this  law in DC band handguns and effectively  banned the use of long guns shotguns  rifles for self-defense and that  immediately sparked a protest by a young  first term congressman from Texas named  Ron Paul and it turns out he was  successful he got the law drafted he got  the law passed from both houses of  Congress got President Gerald Ford to  sign it however because of some drafting  ambiguities the DC government read the  law only to apply to future changes in  their gun laws and not to pass changes  in their gun laws and so they basically  just ignored that federal law well thank  you so much again for coming in fairies  on TV I'm Tracy Oppenheimer

